  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Locally heavy rain possible Sunday into Monday,\nwith an active weather pattern expected much of next week.\n\nThe main system of interest is an expansive upper low spinning\nacross Indiana. This broad cyclonic circulation is forecast to \npush eastward over the next few days which will lend itself \ntoward an increasingly active convective pattern into next week.\n\nThe initial surface pattern has not changed a whole lot in the\npast 24 hours. A frontal zone remains stalled near Savannah,\nGeorgia while evolving into a warm front across the Mid-South.\nThis frontal system extends into an occluded low which\naccompanies the vertically stacked upper low over Illinois. High\npressure over the Mid-Atlantic region has largely filled,\nalthough gradients still remain weak. Winds have shifted over to\nsouth to southeasterly which has aided in an uptick in dew\npoints/humidity levels. \n\nFor tonight, showers increase in coverage for the Blue Ridge \nwestward. Forecast lows fall into the 60s to low 70s (mid 70s \nfor the metro hubs).\n\nBy Sunday, the influence of the upstream closed low begins to\nenter the picture as it pushes along the Ohio River. While\nevolving into more of an open wave, this eventual positively-\ntilted trough will begin to lower heights across areas west of\nI-95. As these modest height falls move in during peak heating,\nthese locations would offer the most likely opportunity for\nstrong to severe thunderstorms. The increasingly moist\nenvironment will favor abundant low clouds which would curb\ninstability levels. Any cloud breaks would offer the best\nopportunity for destabilization. Although downdraft CAPE levels\nare unimpressive, the fact precipitable water values rise to 2\nto 2.50 inches will favor water-loaded downdrafts capable of \nstrong and gusty winds. With this tropical air mass entering the\npicture, cannot fully rule out the threat for flash flooding \ndespite the ongoing drought. A number of 12Z high-resolution \nmodels show north/south oriented patches of 3 to 5 inches, \nalbeit in different spots from one another. With abundant clouds\nand showers in the area, highs will drop down into the upper \n70s to mid 80s.\n\nModels agree on a bit more eastward acceleration of the upper\ntrough on Monday. A meridional (north/south) oriented frontal \nzone sits off to the west as abundant modified tropical moisture\nstreams poleward. This generally focuses the upper-echilon \nmoisture content to east of the Blue Ridge. Models still favor \nprecipitable water values on the order of the 95th to 99th \npercentile based on early August climatology. Numerical guidance\nis all over the place in terms of precipitation placement. \nHowever, the environment certainly supports torrential \ndownpours. Any flash flood threat will be dependent on: (1) \nWhich areas see heavy rainfall the day before, (2) If repeat \nconvection occurs, and (3) Whether vulnerable urbanized/poor \ndrainage locations are impacted. For now, will continue to \nmonitor the situation. Expect highs to push back into the mid \n80s, with some overnight fog possible where heavier rain falls.\n\nShower and thunderstorm chances continue each afternoon and evening \nas we sit within the warm sector. Meanwhile, waves of low pressure \nwill continue to pivot through as a stalled boundary sits just south \nand east of the area. Ample moisture will yield efficient rain \nproducing storms and oppressive humidity values for the middle and \nend of next week. This is due largely in part to no significant \nfront to flip over the airmass.\n\nThe greatest concentration of storms each day will likely be over \nthe terrain and in/around where the bay/river breeze sets up. \nOverall coverage will remain scattered, but likely amplified during \nthe peak heating period given the high PWATS (1.5-2\"+) sitting over \nthe region. Any flood threat would remain relegated to poor drainage \nareas pending repetitive rounds of storms or if storm track/sit over \nan urban location. Highs get back close to 90 degrees Tuesday \nthrough Saturday next week. Overnight lows will fall back into \nthe mid to upper 60s.",
